4
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:12,620
the quicksort is even
a little bit faster,

5
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:15,090
of an n log n sorting algorithm.

6
00:00:15,090 --> 00:00:16,940
It's similar to the
merge sort, in that it

7
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:20,090
uses divide and conquer
and that it uses recursion.

8
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:24,470
Meaning, it splits up the list
into smaller parts, sorts them,

9
00:00:24,470 --> 00:00:27,530
and then returns them
back into the larger

10
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:29,303
list, which is now sorted.

11
00:00:29,303 --> 00:00:31,470
But it's a little bit
different than the merge sort.

12
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:34,520
The merge sort, by comparison,
divides up the list

13
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:37,760
into buckets of size 1
and then merges them back

14
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:39,680
into a sorted list.

15
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:44,540
From the top-down, it goes top,
the top being the unsorted list,

16
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:47,180
divides it down into
buckets of size 1,

17
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:51,270
and then merges them back
up into a sorted list.

18
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:53,300
Well, the quicksort,
it actually--

19
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:56,510
just think of it from that
top-down approach again.

20
00:00:56,510 --> 00:01:00,350
It sorts as it goes down, so it
doesn't-- by the time it divides

21
00:01:00,350 --> 00:01:02,730
everything up,
it's done sorting.

22
00:01:02,730 --> 00:01:04,540
And it does that using a pivot.

23
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:06,240
So here's how the pivot works.

24
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:08,290
A pivot is a number in the list.

25
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:11,250
And what basically
happens is things

26
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:13,650
are moved around in the
list so that everything

27
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:16,060
that's smaller than the
pivot goes on the left.

28
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:19,650
Everything that's right--
that's larger than the pivot

29
00:01:19,650 --> 00:01:21,100
goes on the right.

30
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:23,280
And so then once you're
done moving everything

31
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:26,650
around the pivot, you haven't
sorted the entire list.

32
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:29,460
You've only sorted
everything based on a pivot.

33
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:31,050
Smaller than the
pivot on the left.

34
00:01:31,050 --> 00:01:32,650
Greater than the
pivot on the right.

35
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:35,970
Well, then this is where the
power of recursion comes in.

36
00:01:35,970 --> 00:01:38,610
If I know everything on the
left is smaller than the pivot,

37
00:01:38,610 --> 00:01:41,310
then that's my new
sublist to sort.

38
00:01:41,310 --> 00:01:44,130
It's still unsorted, but
it's much smaller now.

39
00:01:44,130 --> 00:01:47,760
I take the new smaller than
the pivot list and call

40
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:50,085
quicksort on that list.

41
00:01:50,085 --> 00:01:52,560
And I do that again and
again until the entire list

42
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:54,950
is broken down into pieces
that are sorted now.

